Azure AD Object ID Lookup PowerShell Script
Overview
This PowerShell script retrieves the Display Name and Object Type for Azure AD / Microsoft Entra ID Object IDs provided in a CSV file.

The script:

Connects to Azure using Connect-AzAccount.
Reads Object IDs from an input CSV file.
Checks each Object ID against Azure AD users.
If the Object ID is not a user, checks whether it belongs to an Azure AD group.
Identifies objects that cannot be found.
Exports the results to a CSV file.
Prerequisites
Before running the script, ensure the following are available:

PowerShell
Azure PowerShell (Az) module
Appropriate permissions to read users and groups in Microsoft Entra ID
Access to the Azure tenant
If the Az module is not installed, install it using:

Install-Module Az -Scope CurrentUser

Verify that the required commands are available:

Get-Command Connect-AzAccount
Get-Command Get-AzADUser
Get-Command Get-AzADGroup

Input CSV Format
The input CSV must contain a column named:

ObjectId

Example:

ObjectId
11111111-1111-1111-1111-111111111111
22222222-2222-2222-2222-222222222222
33333333-3333-3333-3333-333333333333

The ObjectId column should contain the Microsoft Entra ID Object ID of a user or group.

File Paths
By default, the script expects the input file at:

C:\Temp\ObjectIds.csv

The results are written to:

C:\Temp\AzureObjects_Output.csv

These paths can be changed in the script:

$InputCsv  = "C:\Temp\ObjectIds.csv"
$OutputCsv = "C:\Temp\AzureObjects_Output.csv"

How the Script Works
For each Object ID, the script first attempts to find a user:

$User = Get-AzADUser -ObjectId $ObjectId -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue

If a user is found, the result is recorded as:

Type = User

If the Object ID is not associated with a user, the script checks for a group:

$Group = Get-AzADGroup -ObjectId $ObjectId -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue

If a group is found, the result is recorded as:

Type = Group

If the Object ID is neither a user nor a group, the script records:

DisplayName = Object Not Found
Type = Unknown

Output CSV
The output CSV contains three columns:

Column	Description
ObjectId	Microsoft Entra ID Object ID
DisplayName	Display name of the user or group
Type	User, Group, or Unknown

Example output:

ObjectId,DisplayName,Type
11111111-1111-1111-1111-111111111111,John Smith,User
22222222-2222-2222-2222-222222222222,Finance Team,Group
33333333-3333-3333-3333-333333333333,Object Not Found,Unknown

Running the Script
Save the PowerShell script as:

Get-AzureObjectDetails.ps1

Place your input CSV at:

C:\Temp\ObjectIds.csv

Run PowerShell and execute:

.\Get-AzureObjectDetails.ps1

The script will prompt for Azure authentication if required.

After completion, the results will be available at:

C:\Temp\AzureObjects_Output.csv

Example
Input
ObjectId
a1234567-1234-1234-1234-123456789abc
b1234567-1234-1234-1234-123456789abc
c1234567-1234-1234-1234-123456789abc

Output
ObjectId,DisplayName,Type
a1234567-1234-1234-1234-123456789abc,John Smith,User
b1234567-1234-1234-1234-123456789abc,IT Administrators,Group
c1234567-1234-1234-1234-123456789abc,Object Not Found,Unknown

Error Handling
The script uses:

-ErrorAction SilentlyContinue

when querying users and groups. This prevents an individual Object ID lookup failure from stopping the entire script.

Blank Object IDs are also skipped:

if ([string]::IsNullOrWhiteSpace($ObjectId)) {
    continue
}

Notes
The script checks users first, followed by groups.
The Object ID must be a valid Microsoft Entra ID Object ID.
The account running the script must have sufficient permissions to read users and groups.
The script does not modify any Azure resources; it performs read-only lookups and creates a local CSV file.
Existing output files with the same name may be overwritten when the script is executed.
